<td id="S4.T4.1.12.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb"><span id="S4.T4.1.12.2.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">28/4</span></td>
<td id="S4.T4.1.12.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb"><span id="S4.T4.1.12.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">162.0<math id="S4.SS2.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{}_{\pm\text{1.4}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo>±</mo><mtext>1.4</mtext></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{}_{\pm\text{1.4}}</annotation></semantics></math></span></td>
<td id="S4.T4.1.12.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb"><span id="S4.T4.1.12.4.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">24.8</span></td></tr>
</table>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption" style="font-size:90%;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Table 4: </span>WMT (<span id="S4.T4.19" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">de-en</span>) test set BLEU score.
Best values for our trained models are <span id="S4.T4.20" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">bolded.</span>
<math id="S4.T4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> refers to number of transformer layers (<math id="S4.T4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{\text{Enc}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>N</mi><mtext>Enc</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{\text{Enc}}</annotation></semantics></math>/<math id="S4.T4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{\text{Dec}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>N</mi><mtext>Dec</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{\text{Dec}}</annotation></semantics></math> for E2D2).
Decoding throughput (<span id="S4.T4.21" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Tput</span>) is measured in tokens / sec on 1 <span id="S4.T4.22" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">H100 80GB</span> machine.
For all models, we use <math id="S4.T4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T=L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T=L</annotation></semantics></math> sampling steps, so the throughput can be higher for diffusion when <math id="S4.T4.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T&lt;L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T&lt;L</annotation></semantics></math>.
We report mean <math id="S4.T4.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pm" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>±</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pm</annotation></semantics></math> standard deviation for 100 samples.
</figcaption>
</figure></div></div>
</figure>
<div id="S4.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Mathematical Reasoning</span> 
Table <a href="#S4.T5" title="Table 5 ‣ 4.2 Results ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Encoder-Decoder Diffusion Language Models for Efficient Training and Inference" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> contains results on the GSM8K benchmark.
As above, E2D2 shows improved downstream performance and decoding throughput compared to diffusion baselines.
Qualitatively, we also observe that E2D2 outperforms BD3LM models; see samples in Appendix <a href="#A7" title="Appendix G Generated Samples ‣ Encoder-Decoder Diffusion Language Models for Efficient Training and Inference" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">G</span></a>.</p>
</div>
