<td id="S4.T7.11.5.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center"><span id="S4.T7.11.5.3.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">45.7<math id="S4.T7.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{}_{\pm\text{0.2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo>±</mo><mtext>0.2</mtext></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{}_{\pm\text{0.2}}</annotation></semantics></math></span></td></tr>
<tr id="S4.T7.11.6" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S4.T7.11.6.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb"><span id="S4.T7.11.6.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">2</span></td>
<td id="S4.T7.11.6.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb"><span id="S4.T7.11.6.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">50.1</span></td>
<td id="S4.T7.11.6.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb"><span id="S4.T7.11.6.3.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">34.8<math id="S4.T7.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{}_{\pm\text{0.4}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo>±</mo><mtext>0.4</mtext></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{}_{\pm\text{0.4}}</annotation></semantics></math></span></td></tr>
</table>
</figure>
<div id="S4.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Block size</span> 
For E2D2, using a larger block size increases throughput, as the encoder is invoked fewer times during generation.
However, larger block sizes lead to worse quality, as the likelihood bound is less tight for larger blocks <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>]</cite>.
To explore this trade-off, we fine-tune E2D2 models with <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{\text{Enc}}/N_{\text{Dec}}=28/14" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mtext>Enc</mtext></msub><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mtext>Dec</mtext></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>28</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>14</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{\text{Enc}}/N_{\text{Dec}}=28/14</annotation></semantics></math> and varying block sizes <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S\in\{2,4,8,16,32\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>8</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>16</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>32</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S\in\{2,4,8,16,32\}</annotation></semantics></math> on the GSM8K dataset.
We report results in Table <a href="#S4.T7" title="Table 7 ‣ 4.3 Ablations ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Encoder-Decoder Diffusion Language Models for Efficient Training and Inference" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a> where we observe the anticipated trade-off, with the best quality coming from the smallest block size <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S=2</annotation></semantics></math> and the highest throughput coming from the largest block size <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S=32" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>32</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S=32</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS3.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Number of Diffusion Steps</span> 
In Table <a href="#S4.T8" title="Table 8 ‣ 4.3 Ablations ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Encoder-Decoder Diffusion Language Models for Efficient Training and Inference" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>, we show the effect of diffusion steps per block <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> on sample quality using E2D2 and decoder-only BD3LM.
For both models, we use <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S=4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S=4</annotation></semantics></math>.
E2D2 outperforms BD3LM in quality for each <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>.
As <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> decreases, relative throughput gains from E2D2 diminish, since the decoder requires fewer invocations.
For <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T=1</annotation></semantics></math>, E2D2 achieves comparable throughput to the 16-layer BD3LM because every sampling step invokes both the encoder and decoder, which together use comparable FLOPs relative to the 16-layer BD3LM.
The 12-layer BD3LM achieves better throughput than E2D2 for <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T=1</annotation></semantics></math>, but sacrifices quality.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.T8" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering" style="font-size:90%;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 8: </span>Performance and decoding speed trade-off across varying number of diffusion steps <math id="S4.T8.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> for BD3LM and E2D2 on WMT.
Decoding throughput (<span id="S4.T8.13" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Tput</span>) is measured in tokens / sec on 1 <span id="S4.T8.14" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">A100 80GB</span> machine. Note that for all models, we use <math id="S4.T8.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> total sampling steps, so the throughput for diffusion models can in practice increase when <math id="S4.T8.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T&lt;S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T&lt;S</annotation></semantics></math> for block size <math id="S4.T8.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>.
We report mean <math id="S4.T8.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pm" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>±</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pm</annotation></semantics></math> standard deviation for 100 samples.
Best BLEU and throughput numbers are bolded.
</figcaption>
